[38;5;231m⏺[39m Here's what I found: the repo is one static [38;5;153mindex.html[39m with an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m and a single commit. There's no task
  model, no storage, no users, no server, no build tooling — nothing.

  That matters for scoping. "Notify users when tasks they care about change" assumes four things that don't exist yet:

  - [1mTasks[0m — there's no task data at all, just a heading
  - [1mUsers[0m — no accounts, no identity, no sessions
  - [1mChange events[0m — nothing produces a "task changed" signal
  - [1mDelivery[0m — no server, so no push, email, or background anything

  A notifications system is the layer on top of those. Built against nothing, it would end up defining the task model
  and the user model by accident, which is the expensive way to make those decisions.

  So I'd rather not treat this as one project. My instinct is: tasks + users first, then notifications as its own cycle.
  But that depends on facts I don't have — the repo may not be the whole picture.

